  "question": {
    "text": "Which one of the lanthanoids given below is the most stable in divalent form?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "iKHgp",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Yb</mi></math> (Atomic Number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>70</mn></math>)",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "KCuLk",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>Sm</mo></math> (Atomic Number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>62</mn></math>)",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "XSeDN",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Eu</mi></math> (Atomic Number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>63</mn></math>)",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "PKeEc",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ce</mi></math> (Atomic Number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>58</mn></math>)",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ce</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><mfenced><mi>xe</mi></mfenced><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Eu</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><mfenced><mi>xe</mi></mfenced><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Sm</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><mfenced><mi>xe</mi></mfenced><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Yb</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>:</mo><mfenced><mi>xe</mi></mfenced><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mn>14</mn></msup></math></p><p>As&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Yb</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Eu</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;has completely filled and half filled&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi></math>&nbsp;orbital, so they&nbsp;would be the most stable. But among the these two&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Eu</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;is more stable based on reduction potential value.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>&#176;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#8658;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>Eu</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>Yb</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>35</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>05</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p><p>Hence, due to more reduction potential in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Eu</mi></math> as compared to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Yb</mi></math>, it can concluded that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Eu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> is more stable than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Yb</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>.</p>",
